Subject: Model comparison with restricted cubic splines
Message-ID: <54794986-F8CA-4179-B7C7-8CDB99C351E7@tufts.edu>

Hello,

I have a really really basic question that I can't seem to figure out.  I'm interested in determining whether a non-linear predictor (using restricted cubic splines) is warranted in my model.  For a predictor X, how do I determine using lmer whether rcs(X,3) is significantly better than just X?

Thank you,
Ariel

From morgan-kRQskCh0V2e+fvpajLlQkQ at public.gmane.org  Thu Feb  3 19:50:40 2011
From: morgan-kRQskCh0V2e+fvpajLlQkQ at public.gmane.org (Morgan Sonderegger)
Date: Thu, 03 Feb 2011 13:50:40 -0600
Subject: Re: Model comparison with restricted cubic splines
In-Reply-To: <54794986-F8CA-4179-B7C7-8CDB99C351E7@tufts.edu>
References: <54794986-F8CA-4179-B7C7-8CDB99C351E7@tufts.edu>
Message-ID: <AANLkTikUHMFBhRBBHw9g=G-UQz1NZ5jHsyO2WZ7p4gpx@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Ariel,

rcs(X,3) consists of two "orthogonal basis functions" of X: the first is X
itself, and the second is a nonlinear function of X.  (The details of rcs
are given in Harrell 2001, but I haven't been able to find them online.)

Hence, rcs(X,3) is a superset of X, and you can use anova to perform a
likelihood ratio test, to see if the inclusion of the non-linear term is
warranted.  For example:

mod1 <- lm( y ~ x)
mod2 <- lm(y ~ rcs(x,3))

anova(mod1,mod2)

If the resulting p-value falls below your threshold for significance (0.05,
0.01..), you can conclude that the extra term is justified.

Morgan





