On Sun, Feb 20, 2011 at 4:32 PM, Klinton Bicknell <kbicknell-XkckGZ689+c at public.gmane.org>wrote:

> One other issue I'd like to flag in this discussion of best practices is
> that, in my experience, it's a relatively common occurrence that a model
> with the full random effects structure [(A*B | ppt)+(A*B | item)] fails to
> converge -- and certainly this is even more frequently the case in slightly
> more complicated factorial designs (e.g., 2 x 3). When the full model fails
> to converge, of course, the likelihood ratio test isn't valid, and so the
> model selection problem becomes still less straightforward.
>

Hi Klinton,

I think I mentioned this case in my email. In those cases, it's, however,
usually the case that the more complex model isn't justified (I think in PL
experiments, the most common reason for the failure to converge is that the
random effect (co)variances can't be distinguished from zero; or, for logit
models, that the model is overfit, leading to zeros in to many of the design
cells).


> The approach I've often taken in these cases is to examine the random
> effect estimates of the full model that didn't converge, and to
> (iteratively) remove the random effect(s) with the lowest estimated variance
> (while respecting constraints such as never having random slopes for
> interactions without also having the random slopes for main effects, etc.)
> until the model converges. But I'm certainly not convinced this is the best
> solution.
>

That's indeed what I suggest in the blog post that I linked to my email
(gain: http://hlplab.wordpress.com/2009/05/14/random-effect-structure/). You
right that there is more to it though, I believe (for the reasons given
above), that this procedure will often/usually be justified. I hope that
future versions of lmer will have more interpretable error messages to make
it easier for users like us to interpret what to do next.

Florian


